  "enhanced_information": "The clinical presentation of asthenia, drowsiness, paresthesias, gait disorder, and loss of strength and sensation in the lower extremities, combined with laboratory findings of macrocytic anemia (MCV 107 fL), low haptoglobin, high LDH, and negative Coombs test, strongly suggests vitamin B12 deficiency. This condition is known to cause both neurological symptoms and macrocytic anemia due to ineffective erythropoiesis. The retrieved evidence supports the association of vitamin B12 with the treatment of macrocytic anemia and its role in neuropathy. The standard treatment for vitamin B12 deficiency, especially with neurological involvement, is parenteral vitamin B12 administration. Other options such as folic acid and splenectomy, oral corticosteroids, or plasma exchange do not address the underlying deficiency and are not indicated in this scenario. Therefore, the most appropriate treatment is parenteral vitamin B12.",
